"""Complete the spinal-cord chapter with vascular, nutritional and SCI care."""
from create_spinal_cord import rows,q,SC,DC,CE
from qbank_authoring import ref,finalize
NO=ref('Nitrous Oxide-Induced Subacute Combined Degeneration: Diagnosis and Treatment','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10313972/')
AD=ref('Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine: Autonomic Dysreflexia Guideline','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8152175/')
AV=ref('Spinal Dorsal Intradural AV Fistulas: Natural History, Imaging and Management','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10516273/')
SD=ref('Subacute Combined Degeneration of the Spinal Cord','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK560728/')
NM=ref('NEMOS Diagnostic and Differential Recommendations','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10267280/')

q('MRI incidentally shows cervical cord compression, but there are no myelopathic signs or radicular symptoms. What is the appropriate general principle?', 'Clinical counseling and follow-up rather than automatic prophylactic surgery solely from the image',[
 ('Every compressed image mandates emergency surgery','The clinical syndrome and risk profile matter.'),('No counseling is needed because progression is impossible','Patients should know relevant symptoms and follow-up needs.'),('A scan alone establishes the degree of functional disability','Disability requires clinical assessment.')],
 'Radiological compression and clinical myelopathy differ', '**The DCM guideline does not recommend routine prophylactic surgery for nonmyelopathic compression without radiculopathy.**', ['Radiculopathy can increase progression risk and changes counseling.','New gait or hand signs require reassessment.'], '**Provide a clear monitoring plan.**', ['Discuss warning symptoms.','Avoid both image-driven overtreatment and dismissal of new deficits.'], [['Image','Compression'],['Clinical syndrome','May be absent'],['Management','Risk-informed follow-up']], [DC])
q('A patient with a cervical syrinx has bilateral cape-like pain-temperature loss but preserved vibration and position sense. Which structure is initially affected?', 'Crossing spinothalamic fibers in the anterior white commissure',[
 ('Posterior columns alone','These mediate vibration and proprioception.'),('Optic chiasm','This does not explain segmental truncal/arm sensory loss.'),('A single peripheral small nerve','This does not create the suspended bilateral distribution.')],
 'A central cavity can selectively interrupt crossing fibers', '**Syringomyelia can produce dissociated segmental sensory loss.**', ['Expansion can affect anterior horn cells and long tracts.','Chiari malformation and other structural causes should be considered.'], '**Image the relevant neuraxis.**', ['Assess weakness, pain and progression.','The underlying cause determines treatment.'], [['Initial pathway','Crossing pain-temperature fibers'],['Preserved early','Posterior-column modalities'],['Later expansion','Motor/long-tract involvement']], [SC], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Spastic gait and impaired vibration/position sense coexist with distal neuropathic findings in a patient with B12 deficiency. Which syndrome best describes the cord component?', 'Subacute combined degeneration',[
 ('Pure anterior horn disease','This does not explain posterior-column sensory loss.'),('Isolated vestibular disease','This does not account for pyramidal signs and position-sense loss.'),('A pure motor endplate disorder','That preserves sensation.')],
 'A combined tract and peripheral syndrome can have one nutritional cause', '**Posterior and lateral cord pathways can be affected in B12 deficiency.**', ['Peripheral nerve involvement can coexist.','Macrocytic anemia is not mandatory.'], '**Treat promptly and identify the cause.**', ['Malabsorption and autoimmune gastritis are relevant possibilities.','Recovery may be incomplete if treatment is delayed.'], [['Posterior columns','Position/vibration loss'],['Lateral pathways','Pyramidal signs'],['Peripheral nerves','May also be involved']], [SD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A myeloneuropathy resembles B12 deficiency after gastric surgery, but B12 studies are adequate and zinc exposure is excessive. Which deficiency deserves assessment?', 'Copper deficiency',[
 ('Pyridoxine deficiency is proven by the cord MRI alone','The pattern and exposures particularly suggest copper deficiency.'),('Normal B12 excludes every nutritional cord disorder','Copper and other causes can mimic it.'),('An immune motor neuropathy alone explains the sensory level','A cord-plus-peripheral syndrome requires broader localization.')],
 'Nutritional myelopathy is not limited to B12', '**Copper deficiency can produce posterior-column and corticospinal dysfunction.**', ['Malabsorption and excess zinc are important clues.','Anemia or neutropenia may coexist.'], '**Confirm and correct the cause.**', ['Review supplements and gastrointestinal history.','Neurological recovery may be limited despite biochemical correction.'], [['Risk','Gastric surgery/malabsorption'],['Exposure','Excess zinc'],['Pattern','B12-like myeloneuropathy']], [SD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A young adult uses nitrous oxide heavily and develops sensory ataxia with posterior-column MRI changes despite a low-normal serum B12. What is the best interpretation?', 'Functional B12 inactivation can occur; assess methylmalonic acid or homocysteine and treat the compatible syndrome promptly',[
 ('A normal-range serum B12 excludes toxicity','Serum concentration can be misleading.'),('Continued nitrous oxide use is safe if oral vitamins are added','Ongoing exposure can prevent recovery and worsen injury.'),('Only a brain tumor can explain the symptoms','The exposure and myeloneuropathy pattern provide a specific alternative.')],
 'Measured concentration and functional activity can diverge', '**Nitrous oxide inactivates B12-dependent pathways.**', ['Metabolic markers can support functional deficiency.','Obtain samples promptly when possible without delaying treatment.'], '**Stop exposure and arrange appropriate parenteral B12 treatment.**', ['Provide substance-use support and follow-up.','Assess alternative or coexisting nutritional problems.'], [['Serum B12','Can appear adequate'],['MMA/homocysteine','Functional markers'],['Essential action','Exposure cessation and treatment']], [NO], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Paraplegia reaches maximal severity within minutes during an aortic vascular event. Which cause is most likely?', 'Spinal cord ischemia',[
 ('Typical nutritional myelopathy','That usually evolves more slowly.'),('A routine chronic degenerative neuropathy','This does not explain the hyperacute cord syndrome.'),('Inflammatory myelitis proven solely by a T2 lesion','Tempo and vascular context favor ischemia.')],
 'Time to nadir is diagnostically powerful', '**A hyperacute deficit with vascular or aortic context raises spinal infarction.**', ['Pain and an anterior cord pattern may occur.','Early imaging can be nondiagnostic.'], '**Exclude compression and assess the vascular emergency.**', ['Manage perfusion and the underlying cause through an appropriate team.','Do not assume every long T2 lesion is inflammatory.'], [['Minutes to nadir','Vascular clue'],['Aortic context','Important association'],['MRI','May evolve']], [SC], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Can a normal early spinal MRI completely exclude spinal cord infarction?', 'No; abnormalities may emerge later and repeat imaging can be needed when the clinical syndrome remains convincing',[
 ('Yes, every infarct is conspicuous immediately','Early imaging sensitivity is imperfect.'),('No, therefore imaging should never be performed','Imaging is essential to exclude compression and other causes.'),('A normal scan proves functional symptoms','This conclusion is not justified by an early negative study.')],
 'Imaging evolves after tissue injury', '**The clinical tempo and examination remain important in early suspected ischemia.**', ['Diffusion imaging can help but has technical limitations in the cord.','Repeat studies may clarify the lesion.'], '**Continue evaluating urgent alternatives.**', ['Exclude vascular, compressive and inflammatory causes as appropriate.','Reassess the working diagnosis as new evidence appears.'], [['Early MRI','Can be negative'],['Repeat MRI','May reveal injury'],['Clinical localization','Still essential']], [SC])
q('An older man has progressive gait and bladder dysfunction with long-segment cord edema and serpiginous perimedullary flow voids. Which diagnosis is especially important?', 'Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula causing venous congestive myelopathy',[
 ('Idiopathic inflammatory myelitis established by edema alone','The vascular surface vessels are a major alternative clue.'),('An isolated lumbar root lesion','This does not explain the intramedullary long-segment pattern.'),('Pure ALS','Sensory/autonomic cord findings and vascular imaging point elsewhere.')],
 'Venous hypertension can mimic inflammatory myelopathy', '**A fistula can impair cord venous drainage and produce progressive dysfunction.**', ['Symptoms can fluctuate with activity.','Prominent perimedullary vessels and edema are important but must be interpreted carefully.'], '**Arrange targeted vascular assessment.**', ['Spinal angiography is used for definitive localization.','Endovascular or surgical closure is selected according to the anatomy.'], [['Mechanism','Venous congestion'],['MRI clue','Edema plus abnormal surface vessels'],['Confirmation','Spinal vascular imaging/angiography']], [AV], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why should spinal compression be excluded before labeling an acute cord syndrome idiopathic transverse myelitis?', 'A structural emergency can mimic inflammatory myelitis and requires a different time-critical treatment',[
 ('All cord T2 lesions are autoimmune','Compression, ischemia and metabolic causes can produce signal change.'),('Steroid response always proves idiopathic inflammation','Response is not uniquely diagnostic.'),('CSF testing replaces MRI in every patient','Anatomical imaging is needed to assess compression.')],
 'Myelitis is an etiological conclusion, not a synonym for weakness', '**The differential includes structural, vascular, infectious and immune causes.**', ['MRI distribution and enhancement help refine it.','AQP4 and MOG testing can reclassify selected inflammatory cases.'], '**Treat the identified mechanism.**', ['Do not delay decompression for a routine immune panel.','Use CSF and other tests after appropriate safety and anatomical assessment.'], [['Cord syndrome','Localization'],['Myelitis','Inflammatory attribution'],['Compression','Urgent alternative']], [SC,NM])
q('A long central cord lesion is found during severe myelitis. Which statement is most accurate?', 'Lesion length supports a focused differential but does not distinguish NMOSD, MOGAD, sarcoidosis and other causes by itself',[
 ('At least three segments proves AQP4 positivity','The pattern is not antigen-specific.'),('A long lesion excludes vascular disease','Vascular and venous causes can be long.'),('A short lesion always excludes NMOSD','Timing and disease evolution can affect apparent length.')],
 'A pattern changes probability rather than identifying one antibody', '**LETM has inflammatory, infectious, vascular and structural mimics.**', ['Assess optic, brainstem and systemic features.','Obtain appropriate serum cell-based testing when indicated.'], '**Reconcile laboratory and imaging evidence.**', ['A negative or low-positive result must be interpreted with the phenotype.','Consider repeat or alternative assessment for discordant cases.'], [['Long lesion','Broad focused differential'],['Antibody','Etiological evidence'],['Systemic context','Can identify alternatives']], [NM,AV])
q('Hypotension with bradycardia follows a high spinal injury. What diagnosis should be considered after assessing bleeding and other causes?', 'Neurogenic shock from loss of sympathetic control',[
 ('Spinal shock is the identical hemodynamic definition','Spinal shock refers to depressed neurological reflex activity.'),('Hemorrhage is impossible in an injured patient with bradycardia','Bleeding must still be assessed.'),('A normal spinal injury cannot affect vascular tone','High cord injury can impair sympathetic regulation.')],
 'Autonomic disconnection can alter circulation', '**Neurogenic shock may produce vasodilation and relative bradycardia.**', ['Trauma patients can have simultaneous hemorrhagic shock.','Hypoxia and other causes of bradycardia also need assessment.'], '**Support perfusion through an acute spinal-injury protocol.**', ['Use appropriate fluids and vasoactive treatment under specialist care.','Avoid conflating absent reflexes with the cause of hypotension.'], [['Neurogenic shock','Hemodynamic autonomic failure'],['Spinal shock','Neurological reflex suppression'],['Trauma assessment','Still exclude bleeding']], [SC], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with a chronic T4 injury develops pounding headache, flushing above the lesion and a sudden marked rise in blood pressure. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Autonomic dysreflexia',[
 ('Neurogenic shock','That is generally a hypotensive syndrome.'),('Simple baseline hypertension without trigger assessment','The abrupt characteristic syndrome requires urgent evaluation.'),('A normal inevitable episode requiring no action','Severe hypertension can cause major complications.')],
 'A below-lesion trigger can cause an above-lesion crisis', '**Autonomic dysreflexia is particularly associated with injuries at or above T6.**', ['Bladder distension is a common trigger.','Bradycardia may occur but is not mandatory.'], '**Treat as an emergency.**', ['Sit upright if safe, monitor frequently and remove the trigger.','Use rapid-onset short-duration antihypertensive treatment when indicated.'], [['Risk level','Usually T6 or above'],['Trigger','Often bladder/bowel'],['Danger','Acute severe hypertension']], [AD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What should be checked promptly during autonomic dysreflexia in a catheterized patient?', 'Catheter obstruction and bladder distension while managing blood pressure and positioning',[
 ('Only serum cholesterol','This does not identify the acute trigger.'),('Routine elective MRI before addressing the bladder','Trigger removal and acute BP management should not be delayed.'),('Forceful bladder compression without precautions','This can worsen stimulation and harm.')],
 'Remove the noxious input as well as treating the pressure', '**A blocked urinary drainage system is a common reversible cause.**', ['Inspect tubing and drainage according to the protocol.','Bowel impaction, skin injury and other triggers should be considered if the bladder is not responsible.'], '**Avoid creating further stimulation.**', ['Use appropriate local measures and medication precautions.','Continue BP monitoring after the trigger is relieved.'], [['First trigger search','Urinary system'],['Other triggers','Bowel, skin and procedures'],['Follow-up','Watch for recurrent or low BP']], [AD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why can a cervical cord injury threaten ventilation even without primary lung disease?', 'It can impair diaphragmatic, intercostal and cough-related motor pathways according to the injury level',[
 ('The cord has no role in respiratory muscle control','Respiratory motor pathways traverse the cord.'),('Only oxygen diffusion can cause respiratory failure','Neuromuscular ventilatory failure is another mechanism.'),('Normal initial saturation guarantees stable ventilation','Reserve can decline and clearance problems can develop.')],
 'Level predicts respiratory motor risk', '**Phrenic motor supply arises predominantly from C3–C5.**', ['Lower cervical and thoracic pathways contribute to intercostal and expiratory function.','Weak cough increases secretion retention risk.'], '**Assess ventilation and clearance separately.**', ['Use serial respiratory evaluation in significant injuries.','Plan support and airway clearance before deterioration.'], [['C3–C5','Phrenic/diaphragm supply'],['Intercostal/abdominal','Ventilation and cough'],['Monitoring','Reserve and secretions']], [SC])
q('Why is sacral sensory and motor testing important when classifying spinal cord injury?', 'Sacral sparing helps distinguish incomplete from complete injury in the standardized neurological examination',[
 ('Normal arm strength alone determines completeness','Completeness depends on distal sacral function, not arm strength alone.'),('Any leg reflex proves voluntary sacral motor preservation','Reflexes and voluntary function are different.'),('A single MRI image replaces the neurological classification','Imaging and standardized examination provide complementary information.')],
 'Anatomical completeness is a defined clinical classification', '**Assess the relevant sacral sensory and voluntary motor functions using standardized methods.**', ['Reflex activity does not automatically equal preserved voluntary pathways.','The examination can evolve after acute injury.'], '**Repeat and document carefully.**', ['Confounders such as sedation and pain matter.','Classification informs prognosis and rehabilitation without guaranteeing an individual outcome.'], [['Sacral sparing','Incomplete injury clue'],['Reflex','Not identical to voluntary function'],['Assessment','Standardized and repeated']], [SC])
q('After an acute cord disorder, what belongs in comprehensive ongoing care?', 'Bladder/bowel planning, pressure-injury and thrombosis prevention, mobility rehabilitation, pain and autonomic review',[
 ('Only repeat MRI with no functional assessment','Imaging does not address the full disability burden.'),('Permanent bed rest in every recovering patient','Appropriate rehabilitation reduces complications.'),('Ignore sexual and psychological health because the lesion is spinal','These are important aspects of care.')],
 'Cord injury affects several functional systems', '**Preventable complications can cause major additional morbidity.**', ['Assess urinary retention, infection risk and bowel function.','Skin protection and individualized mobility support are central.'], '**Revisit goals and caregiver needs.**', ['Neuropathic pain, spasticity and autonomic symptoms may need specific treatment.','Support participation, communication and psychological health.'], [['Medical prevention','Skin, thrombosis and urinary risks'],['Function','Rehabilitation and equipment'],['Long term','Autonomic and psychosocial care']], [SC,AD])

